+### Editing `server.mjs` — two things that will bite you
+
+`server.mjs` is one ~4.4k-line file holding the server _and_ the whole
+frontend, with zero dependencies and no build step. Two hazards are specific
+to that shape and are not obvious from reading the surrounding code.
+
+**1. The frontend lives inside a template literal, so every backslash needs
+doubling.** The HTML, CSS and browser JS are one big `` const HTML = `…` ``.
+Inside a template literal `\.` collapses to `.`, so a regex written the
+normal way silently loses its escape:
+
+```js
+// in server.mjs source          what the browser actually receives
+/\.([A-Za-z0-9]+)$/          ->  /.([A-Za-z0-9]+)$/     // dot matches ANY char
+/\\.([A-Za-z0-9]+)$/         ->  /\.([A-Za-z0-9]+)$/    // correct
+```
+
+The failure is quiet and plausible-looking: the broken version still matches
+`notes.md` correctly, and only misbehaves on names like `Makefile` (parsed as
+extension `akefile`) or `Xpdf` (parsed as `pdf`). Same rule for backticks and
+`${` in comments — a backtick inside a comment inside the template still ends
+the literal.
+
+`node --check server.mjs` will **not** catch any of this; it only parses the
+outer file. `./install.sh --check` boots the server and re-parses the _served_
+`<script>`, which catches syntax errors but still not a wrong-but-valid regex.
+The only real check is exercising the page in a browser.
+
+**2. `listTmuxSessionNames()` reports every failure as "no sessions".** That is
+what the UI wants — a broken tmux should render as "nothing running" rather
+than an error page — but it means the function cannot tell you whether tmux
+said "nothing is running" or failed to answer at all. Don't build anything
+destructive on it without adding that distinction.
+
+Related, if you ever parse tmux's output: it only prints `no server running`
+when a stale socket file exists. When the socket is gone — every first start
+after a reboot, since `/tmp` is cleared — it prints `error connecting to
+<path> (No such file or directory)` instead. Both mean "no sessions".
+
